WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Did you know that most

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.990
of the time spent
by data scientists

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.000
goes into wrangling data?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.550
More specifically, in
feature engineering,

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.150
which is transforming raw
data into high-quality input

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.160
signals for ML models.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.300
But this process is often
inefficient and brittle.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.650
Well I'm Priyanka,
and in this video

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.920
we will identify
the key challenges

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.760
with feature engineering, how
Vertex Feature Store help solve

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.680
them, and see a quick demo.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.270
Now what are the key
challenges with ML features?

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.560
The first is that they are
hard to share and reuse

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.290
across your different
steps of the ML workflow

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.750
and across projects, which
results in duplicate efforts.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.970
Second is that it is hard to
serve in production reliably

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.280
with lower latency,
and the third

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.820
is that there is an
inadvertent skew in feature

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.040
values between
training and serving,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.870
usually, which causes your model
quality to degrade over time.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.769
That is exactly where Vertex
Feature Store comes in.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.010
It's a fully managed
and unified solution

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.590
to share, discover,
and serve machine

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.920
learning features at scale
across different teams

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.600
within your organization.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.000
And it also helps
reduce the time

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.330
to build and deploy
your AI ML applications

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.730
by making it easy to
manage and organize

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.280
your ML features in one place.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:32.730
It makes the features reusable,
easy to serve, and avoids skew.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.280
Now let's see how to set it up.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.270
In the console, in Vertex
AI, we see the Feature tab.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.360
To get started, let's
click on this documentation

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.900
and explore, using
Feature Store section.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.710
Now the first thing you
need is a Feature Store.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.930
At the time of this
recording, Feature Store

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.000
is in preview so just
know that depending

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.150
on when you're watching this,
there might be more options

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.830
and updates that you would see.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.350
You cannot create a Feature
Store in the console,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.450
so let's use this sample
notebook to learn how to create

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.460
it using the SDK.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.700
This sample uses a movie
recommendations data

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.310
set and the task
is to train a model

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.730
to predict if a user is
going to watch a movie

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.590
and serve this model online.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.790
We will learn to import our
features into Feature Store,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.600
serve online prediction requests
using the imported features,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.930
and then access imported
features in offline jobs,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.390
such as training jobs.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.540
To set up, we install
some additional packages,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.050
set up our project, and
authenticate our Google Cloud

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.170
account.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.760
Step one is to create
data set for output.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.630
We are creating BigQuery data
set to host the output data.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.540
Input the name of the
data set and the table

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.660
we want to store
the output later.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.940
Then we are defining
constants and

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.430
Feature Store-related imports.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.910
Here's how the Vertex
Feature Store actually works.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:07.260
It organizes the data with the
three hierarchical concepts.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.100
Featurestore, which is the
place to store your features--

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.270
EntityType, under Featurestore,
describes an object

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.530
to be modeled, real or
virtual, and Feature itself,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.720
under EntityType, describes an
attribute of that EntityType.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.070
Now in our movie's
prediction example,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.660
we will create a featurestore
called movie_prediction.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.240
This store has
two entity types--

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.220
Users and Movies.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.830
The user's entity
type has age, gender,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.860
and like genres features.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.480
The Movies entity type has
the genre and average rating

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.630
features.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.460
The first thing we do is
to create the featurestore.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.590
The method to create
a featurestore

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.080
returns a long
running operation that

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.360
starts an asynchronous job.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.910
This may take about
three minutes or so,

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.850
and once the
featurestore is created,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.300
we can see it in the console.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.180
And we can create our
entities in this store.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.430
I'm creating two entities here--

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.560
user and movies.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.250
We can also create features
within these entities.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.209
Here I've created age,
gender, and like genres

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:25.410
under user, and title,
genres, and average rating

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.240
under our movies.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.700
If we want, we
can search through

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.610
and filter on these features.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.350
Now we need to import feature
values before we can use them

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.780
for online offline serving.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.370
Let's head back
into our notebook

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.860
to see how to import features
in bulk using the Python SDK.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.790
We define the data source, the
BigQuery table or Cloud Storage

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.060
bucket, and the destination
featurestore, entity

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.430
and the features to be imported.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:01.080
You do this for both users
and the movies entities.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.740
Now for a latency-sensitive
service such as online model

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.070
prediction, we would need
to serve our feature values

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:08.820
online.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.080
For example, for
a movie service,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.110
we might want to
quickly show movies

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:16.080
that the current user
would most likely watch

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.360
by using online predictions.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.270
You can read one
entity per request

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.210
or even read multiple
entities per request.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.510
Now if you need feature
values for high throughput,

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.540
typically for training a
model or batch prediction,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.090
then serving feature
values in batch

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:35.910
is a better idea
than serving online.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:39.870
Consider this example-- if the
task is to prepare a training

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.530
data set to train a model which
predicts if a given user will

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:47.700
watch a given movie, then to
achieve this we need two sets

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:48.450
of inputs--

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.090
features, that we
have already imported,

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.690
and labels, which is the
ground-truth data recorder

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:58.650
that user X has watched
movie Y. It also

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.580
includes the time stamp which
indicates when the ground-truth

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.290
was actually observed.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.710
As labels and feature values
are collected over time,

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.660
those feature values change.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.750
The Feature Store can perform
a point in time lookup

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.460
so that you can
fetch the feature

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.590
values at a particular time.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.020
It's literally the data
version of going back

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.930
to a previous version of
your source code in GitHub.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.940
Imagine freezing the
state of the feature

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.390
values at two
different timestamps.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:31.140
And that was a quick summary
of Vertex Feature Store.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.160
Just know that at the
time of this recording,

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.510
the Feature Store is in
preview, so chances there

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.730
are more options and
features available when

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.200
you're watching this video.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.550
To explore more
up-to-date information,

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.820
check out the documentation
that I've linked below.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.190
Any questions, let me
know in the comments

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.280
or on Twitter @pvergadia.

